Chapter: RangeLetCarry

 A bound proved at a binding must still be proved at its use.

 The emit-boundary prover decides whether a bounded field store needs its
 runtime check. It is handed the field's VALUE expression, not the scope
 around it, so until the range environment was carried on the codegen
 state a `let` between the proof and the use hid the proof: `direct` below
 elided its check and `via-let` -- the same arithmetic through a binding --
 did not.

 Both functions must now report CDX4010. The `.diag` sidecar is the
 instrument: an elision is not visible in the answer, because a program
 whose checks are all emitted computes exactly the same thing, just
 slower. The `.expected` sidecar is the other half -- an elision that
 changes an answer is a wrongly elided check, which is the failure this
 whole path must never have.

Section: Probe

  Small = record {
   s-val : Integer between 0 and 255
  }

  direct : Integer -> Small
  direct (n) = Small { s-val = __narrow (int-mod n 200) }

  via-let : Integer -> Small
  via-let (n) =
   let v = int-mod n 200
   in Small { s-val = __narrow v }

  via-two-lets : Integer -> Small
  via-two-lets (n) =
   let a = int-mod n 100
   in let b = a
   in Small { s-val = __narrow b }

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show ((direct 1234).s-val))
   print-line-uni (show ((via-let 1234).s-val))
   print-line-uni (show ((via-two-lets 1234).s-val))
  end
